# PplwareSexta Video Extractor

**PplwareSexta** is a robust Java desktop utility developed to automate the compilation of YouTube video links from the recurring Friday humor articles ("E Porque Hoje é Sexta") published by the Portuguese technology portal, Pplware.

Instead of forcing users to manually click through multiple sub-pages and copy each embedded URL by hand, this application handles the entire download, scanning, and consolidation workflow in seconds.

---

## What I Developed: Architecture & Features

This project was engineered with modularity and performance choices in mind, providing three distinct execution strategies tailored to different runtime needs:

### 1. Multi-Engine Architecture
*   **`rapidofas` (Concurrent Engine):** Implements multi-threaded page processing. It fires simultaneous HTTP requests to fetch all targeted sub-pages in parallel, drastically reducing the total execution time.
*   **`lentofas` (Sequential Engine):** A reliable, single-threaded fallback implementation that processes pages sequentially, ideal for lightweight environments or controlled network analysis.
*   **`gui` (JavaFX Interface):** A complete visual desktop dashboard that wraps the core extraction logic into an intuitive graphical user interface.

### 2. Graphical User Interface (JavaFX)
*   **Dynamic Parameter Configuration:** Allows the user to easily input the specific article ID, customize the target page range (Start/End page), and dynamically select any local output folder via a native directory browser.
*   **Live Status Logging:** Includes a dedicated visual logging panel that prints real-time operation updates, feedback, and execution checkpoints so the user can follow exactly what the background engines are doing.

### 3. Core Processing Pipeline
*   **Stream Buffering:** Features a specialized I/O buffer layer (`buffer` package) built to intercept raw network streams efficiently.
*   **Character Normalization:** Handles web-specific text encodings through an integrated HTML special characters translation mapping utility.
*   **Automated Deduplication:** Filters incoming links automatically to ensure the final output file contains only unique video references, stripping out accidental duplicates.

> **Language Note:** All internal code comments, variable names, and method structures are written in **Portuguese**. This architectural approach directly mirrors the application's domain, as Pplware is a Portuguese portal and this software was custom-tailored to interact with its native page structures.

---

## Project Structure

The codebase is organized into clean, decoupled packages representing a complete Model-View-Controller (MVC) layout:

```
PplwareSexta/
│
├── docs/
│   └── screenshots/          # Application user interface captures
│
├── help/                     # Developer reference documentation & lookup tables
│   ├── HTML_Special_Characters.txt  # Map used for decoding HTML entity representations
│   └── Queueing.txt                 # Architectural notes regarding thread processing queues
│
├── jars/                     # Compiled executable binary distributions (.jar)
│   ├── PplwareSexta_GUI.jar       # Visual JavaFX desktop app environment
│   ├── PplwareSexta_Lentofas.jar  # Sequential/synchronous CLI tool
│   └── PplwareSexta_Rapidofas.jar # High-speed concurrent CLI tool
│
└── src/                      # Java source code tree
    ├── buffer/               # Low-level network stream input buffering
    ├── gui/                  # Graphical interface layouts (gui.fxml) and Controller event mappings
    ├── lentofas/             # Single-threaded processing execution loop
    ├── rapidofas/            # Multi-threaded thread-pool execution framework
    ├── result/               # Data response structures capturing execution metrics
    └── video/                # Internal business object mapping (Video data model)
```

---

## Prerequisites & Portability

*   **Operating System:** Windows 7, 8, 10, or 11.
*   **Java Environment:** Built for **Java SE 8 (JDK 1.8)** ecosystems.
*   **Portable Launch Option:** Because modern Java runtimes no longer include JavaFX UI modules by default, running the application on newer systems can be tricky. To preserve full portability and avoid installing software system-wide, you can run the JARs using **[jdkPortable (32-bit/64-bit)](https://portableapps.com)** on the PortableApps platform.

A ready-to-use launch script (`jars/PplwareSexta_GUI.bat`) is included to start the desktop experience instantly on Windows environments.

---

## Usage & Interface Layout

### Application Interface (Idle State)
When initialized, the application presents a configuration workspace allowing the declaration of network extraction targets and local destination folders:

![Application Idle](docs/screenshots/before-execution.jpg)

### Processing & Output Verification
Once parameters are set and execution begins, the background parallel operations thread logs real-time status checkpoints directly into the analytical display window:

![Application Running](docs/screenshots/after-execution.jpg)

### Step-by-Step Execution:
1. Launch `jars/PplwareSexta_GUI.jar` (or use the provided `.bat` file).
2. Identify the target article number from Pplware's address bar (e.g., in `.../e-porque-hoje-e-sexta-977/`, the ID is **`977`**).
3. Input **`977`** into the designated parameter field on the screen.
4. Set your page bounds (e.g., Página Inicial 1 to Página Final 2) and click **Pasta** to define where you want the file saved.
5. Click **Iniciar**. The program will spin up the concurrent engine, poll the server, match embeds, clean duplicates, and generate a perfectly formatted `Links.txt` file ready for any standard video utility.

---

## Technical Context & Evolution

Every software project is a product of its time. Built under a native Java 8 stack, the codebase is highly optimized for that ecosystem. If this project were to be transitioned into modern Java 20+ environments in the future, a developer could build upon this solid foundation by exploring a few ecosystem adaptations:
*   Transitioning the raw text matching into DOM selectors using libraries like Jsoup to tolerate layout changes over time.
*   Migrating from legacy explicit thread interrupts (`Thread.stop()`) to cooperative boolean flags or modern JavaFX `Task` cancellation lifecycles.
*   Replacing local file separation strings (`\\`) with cross-platform `java.nio.file.Path` variables to run the engines natively on macOS or Linux systems.
*   Wrapping the manual package references into automated build tools like Maven or Gradle to simplify modern OpenJFX deployment.

